{
 "lead": "An LTI system is <b>causal</b> when its output never depends on future input, and <b>BIBO stable</b> (bounded-input, bounded-output) when every bounded input gives a bounded output. For an LTI system both questions are answered by the impulse response alone. Causality asks where h(t) lives: it must be zero for t<0. Stability asks how big h(t) is overall: the area under |h(t)| must be finite. Only a few universities teach these tests as a chapter of their own, but every syllabus asks them, and designers run them on any filter or control loop before building it.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#convolution-integral-and-its-evaluation\">convolution integral and its evaluation</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Causality: where h(t) lives",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Write the output as y(t)=∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>h(τ)x(t−τ)dτ. The term at τ uses the input at time t−τ. For τ<0 that input lies in the future. The output can be free of future input for every x only if those weights are zero:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "h(t) = 0 for t < 0",
     "where": "the condition is necessary and sufficient for a causal LTI system. h(0) may be nonzero: only negative time is ruled out."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "So e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t) is causal and u(t+1) is not, because it switches on at t=−1 s. A causal h can still be unstable: u(t) is causal with infinite area. A noncausal h can be made causal by delaying it: h(t−T) starts T seconds later. Offline processing (a recorded song, a stored image) may use noncausal filters, because the \"future\" samples are already stored."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A memoryless system is the special case h(t)=Kδ(t): y(t)=Kx(t) depends only on the present input."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "BIBO stability: how big h(t) is",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup> |h(t)| dt < ∞",
     "where": "h(t) is the impulse response and |h(t)| its absolute value. A signal that meets this is called <b>absolutely integrable</b>. The condition is necessary and sufficient for BIBO stability of an LTI system."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Sufficient.</b> Let the input be bounded, |x(t)|≤M<sub>x</sub> for all t. Then |y(t)|≤∫|h(τ)||x(t−τ)|dτ≤M<sub>x</sub>∫|h(τ)|dτ. If the area is finite, every output is bounded by M<sub>x</sub> times the area."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Necessary.</b> Suppose the area is infinite. Choose the bounded input x(t)=sgn[h(−t)], which is +1, −1 or 0. Then y(0)=∫h(τ)x(−τ)dτ=∫|h(τ)|dτ, which is infinite. One bounded input with an unbounded output is enough to fail."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The absolute value matters: without it, positive and negative lobes can cancel and hide an unstable system. When input and output share a unit, h(t) has unit s<sup>−1</sup> and the area is a pure number: the worst-case gain M<sub>y</sub>/M<sub>x</sub>."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "The two tests are independent",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Neither property implies the other. Figure 2.5.1 shows one impulse response of each kind:"
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "causal and stable: e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t), area 1/2;",
      "causal and unstable: u(t), area ∞;",
      "noncausal and stable: e<sup>2t</sup>u(−t), area 1/2;",
      "noncausal and unstable: e<sup>−t</sup>u(−t), which grows without limit as t→−∞, area ∞."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In an exam, run the two tests one after the other: first look at h(t) for t<0, then integrate |h(t)| over all time. For a causal h(t) built from exponentials, the same answer appears in Laplace transform analysis as \"all poles in the left half of the s-plane\"."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "Test h₁(t)=e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t), h₂(t)=e<sup>2t</sup>u(−t) and h₃(t)=u(t) for causality and BIBO stability. Give the area under |h| in each case.",
   "steps": [
    "h₁ is zero for t<0, so it is causal. Area=∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>e<sup>−2t</sup>dt=[−e<sup>−2t</sup>/2]<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>=0−(−1/2)=1/2. Finite, so stable.",
    "h₂ is nonzero for t<0 (for example h₂(−1)=e<sup>−2</sup>=0.135), so it is not causal. Area=∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>0</sup>e<sup>2t</sup>dt=[e<sup>2t</sup>/2]<sub>−∞</sub><sup>0</sup>=1/2−0=1/2. Finite, so stable.",
    "h₃ is zero for t<0, so it is causal. Area=∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>1 dt, which grows without limit. Not stable: the bounded input u(t) gives the ramp y(t)=t·u(t).",
    "Bound check for h₁: an input with |x|≤5 V gives |y|≤5×1/2=2.5 V."
   ],
   "answer": "h₁: causal and stable (area 0.5). h₂: noncausal but stable (area 0.5). h₃: causal but unstable (area ∞)."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Causal does not imply stable: u(t) is zero for t<0 but its area is infinite.",
  "Stable does not imply causal: e<sup>2t</sup>u(−t) has finite area 1/2 but is nonzero before t=0.",
  "Leaving out the absolute value: the ideal low-pass impulse response h(t)=sin(t)/(πt) has net area exactly 1, yet the area under |h(t)| is infinite, so it is not BIBO stable.",
  "Causality allows h(0)≠0; it forbids only t<0. δ(t) itself is causal.",
  "BIBO is tested on h(t), not on one chosen input. A single bounded input that gives a bounded output proves nothing."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the causality condition on h(t) for a continuous-time LTI system.",
   "marks": 1,
   "a": "h(t)=0 for t<0."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the BIBO stability condition on h(t).",
   "marks": 1,
   "a": "∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>|h(t)|dt<∞ (h absolutely integrable)."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is u(t) causal but not BIBO stable? Name a bounded input that shows it.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "u(t)=0 for t<0, so it is causal; ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>1 dt is infinite, so it is unstable. The bounded input u(t) gives y(t)=t·u(t), which grows without bound."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "For h(t)=3e<sup>−4t</sup>u(t), test causality and find the area under |h(t)|.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Zero for t<0, so causal. Area=3∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>e<sup>−4t</sup>dt=3/4=0.75, finite, so stable."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "For h(t)=e<sup>3t</sup>u(−t), test causality and find the area under |h(t)|.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Nonzero for t<0, so not causal. Area=∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>0</sup>e<sup>3t</sup>dt=1/3≈0.333, finite, so stable."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "For h(t)=e<sup>−t</sup>u(t)+2e<sup>2t</sup>u(−t), test causality and stability and give the area.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "The second term is nonzero for t<0, so the system is not causal. Area=∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>e<sup>−t</sup>dt+2∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>0</sup>e<sup>2t</sup>dt=1+2×(1/2)=2, finite, so stable."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. For systems described by transfer functions, stability is read from pole positions (left half of the s-plane, or inside the unit circle for discrete time), and control engineers add gain and phase margins to say how far a loop is from instability. The same area of |h| bounds the worst-case error of a digital filter. Search for \"BIBO stability\", \"L1 norm of impulse response\" and \"absolute summability\".",
